                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1228,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes one position along one dimension - one row, one column, one frame - and that dimension disappears.\n\nt.select 0 3 on a 10 x 10 tensor is row 3: a 1-D tensor of 10. t.select 1 3 is column 3, also a 1-D tensor of 10. Use it to pull one joint, one channel or one frame out of a larger tensor when you want the thing itself rather than a tensor with a single row in it.\n\nselect VERSUS index_select:\nTaking row 3 with t.select gives shape (10) - the row dimension is gone, because a single row has no row dimension left. Taking rows [3] with t.index_select gives shape (1, 10): still a matrix, with one row in it. If something downstream expects a matrix, use t.index_select or t[] 3:4;\nif you want the vector itself, t.select gets it with no squeeze.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.select <dim: int> <index: int>\nDefaults: dim 0, index 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.select 1 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension to take from: 0 rows, 1 columns, and so on. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last dimension).\n\nindex (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich position along it. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last).\n\nChanging dim or index does NOT send by itself - the next tensor uses them. In the demo a metro inside make_numbered re-sends the source ten times a second, so the picture follows your edits.\n\nIf dim or index is out of range for the tensor, nothing is sent and nothing is said.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe slice, with one dimension fewer than the input. A 1-D input gives a single value (a tensor with no dimensions). It is a view of the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.select 0 3 takes row 3: the strip shows 30 to 39. Set dim to 1 and it is column 3 instead - 3, 13, 23 ... 93 - still laid out as one row, because it is now a plain 1-D tensor with no memory of having been a column. t.info shows the shape: [10] either way.\n\nRELATED:\nt[] does the same with a typed index (3 or :,3), and can take ranges too. t.index_select takes several positions and keeps the dimension. t.narrow takes a run of positions and keeps the dimension. t.unsqueeze puts a removed dimension back. np.[] is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes one position along one dimension - one row, one column, one frame - and that dimension disappears.\n\nt.select 0 3 on a 10 x 10 tensor is row 3: a 1-D tensor of 10. t.select 1 3 is column 3, also a 1-D tensor of 10. Use it to pull one joint, one channel or one frame out of a larger tensor when you want the thing itself rather than a tensor with a single row in it.\n\nselect VERSUS index_select:\nTaking row 3 with t.select gives shape (10) - the row dimension is gone, because a single row has no row dimension left. Taking rows [3] with t.index_select gives shape (1, 10): still a matrix, with one row in it. If something downstream expects a matrix, use t.index_select or t[] 3:4;\nif you want the vector itself, t.select gets it with no squeeze.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.select <dim: int> <index: int>\nDefaults: dim 0, index 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.select 1 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension to take from: 0 rows, 1 columns, and so on. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last dimension).\n\nindex (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich position along it. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last).\n\nChanging dim or index does NOT send by itself - the next tensor uses them. In the demo a metro inside make_numbered re-sends the source ten times a second, so the picture follows your edits.\n\nIf dim or index is out of range for the tensor, nothing is sent and nothing is said.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe slice, with one dimension fewer than the input. A 1-D input gives a single value (a tensor with no dimensions). It is a view of the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.select 0 3 takes row 3: the strip shows 30 to 39. Set dim to 1 and it is column 3 instead - 3, 13, 23 ... 93 - still laid out as one row, because it is now a plain 1-D tensor with no memory of having been a column. t.info shows the shape: [10] either way.\n\nRELATED:\nt[] does the same with a typed index (3 or :,3), and can take ranges too. t.index_select takes several positions and keeps the dimension. t.narrow takes a run of positions and keeps the dimension. t.unsqueeze puts a removed dimension back. np.[] is the numpy version."
                },
